<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A selector field is made of:

- A label naming the value, in plain words.
- An optional hint, which should come before the field.
- The input that the selector draws.
- An error message when the value is not accepted.

The selector wrapper adds no label, role or text of its own. The input it draws supplies its own name, so check each field you use.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-selector"><code>ha-selector</code></h3>

The wrapper. A selector is written in YAML as a mapping with one key, the type, and that type’s options.

```yaml
selector:
  number:
    min: 0
    max: 100
    unit_of_measurement: "%"
```

Many types need no options, so the value can be empty: `boolean:`.

The wrapper takes these properties: `selector` (required), `value`, `label`, `helper`, `placeholder`, `disabled`, `required` (on by default), `narrow` and `context`. It fires `value-changed` when the value changes, and the inner input fires the same event. It can check itself with `reportValidity()` and take focus with `focus()`. It has no slots, no styling variables of its own and no role or ARIA attributes.

Older spellings still work: `ui-action` and `ui-color` with a hyphen are read as `ui_action` and `ui_color`, and the old `integration`, `domain`, `device_class`, `manufacturer` and `model` options on the entity and device selectors are read as a `filter`.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The inputs a selector draws take the theme’s text, border and focus colours, font sizes and switch and button sizes. The theme does not restyle selectors separately.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** Which input appears, its layout and its behaviour. Selector options can differ between the automation editor, the dashboard editor and blueprint inputs, so check the form you are working in.

**What needs card-mod.** Nothing: selectors are not in dashboard cards, so there is nothing for card-mod to style.

Each selector below has its YAML key and options.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-action"><code>ha-selector-action</code></h3>

Chooses a list of actions, as in an automation or script. The value is a list of actions.

YAML key `action`. Options: `optionsInSidebar`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-addon"><code>ha-selector-addon</code></h3>

The older name for the app selector. It behaves exactly like `app`: Home Assistant now calls these “apps”, and the editor shows the app picker.

YAML key `addon`. Options: the same as `app`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-app"><code>ha-selector-app</code></h3>

Chooses an installed app. Options `name` and `slug` narrow the list.

YAML key `app`. Options: `name`, `slug`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-area"><code>ha-selector-area</code></h3>

Chooses one or more areas. See [Area Picker](area-picker.html) for the picker it draws. `multiple` allows more than one; `entity` and `device` narrow the list to areas that have a matching entity or device.

YAML key `area`. Options: `entity`, `device`, `multiple`, `reorder`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-areas-display"><code>ha-selector-areas_display</code></h3>

Chooses which areas appear and in what order. The value has `hidden` and `order`, both lists of area IDs.

YAML key `areas_display`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-assist-pipeline"><code>ha-selector-assist_pipeline</code></h3>

Chooses an Assist pipeline, the chain of speech and language steps behind Assist. `include_last_used` adds a “last used” choice.

YAML key `assist_pipeline`. Options: `include_last_used`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-attribute"><code>ha-selector-attribute</code></h3>

Chooses one attribute of an entity. `entity_id` sets which entity, and `hide_attributes` leaves some out.

YAML key `attribute`. Options: `entity_id`, `hide_attributes`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-automation-behavior"><code>ha-selector-automation_behavior</code></h3>

Chooses how a trigger or condition treats several targets, as a set of boxes with a short description under each label. In trigger mode the boxes are `each`, `first` and `all`, and each also shows a picture. In condition mode they are `any` and `all`. `translation_key` takes the wording from an integration’s own translations.

YAML key `automation_behavior`. Options: `mode` (`trigger` or `condition`, with `trigger` the default), `translation_key`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-backup-location"><code>ha-selector-backup_location</code></h3>

Chooses where a backup is stored, from the storage locations Home Assistant knows.

YAML key `backup_location`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-boolean"><code>ha-selector-boolean</code></h3>

A switch for yes or no. The label and any hint sit inside the switch’s own row. Say what “on” means in the label.

YAML key `boolean`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-button-toggle"><code>ha-selector-button_toggle</code></h3>

A short row of buttons, one of which is chosen. `options` lists the choices; `translation_key` and `sort` control their names and order. Use it when there are few choices and all should be visible.

YAML key `button_toggle`. Options: `options`, `translation_key`, `sort`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-choose"><code>ha-selector-choose</code></h3>

Lets people pick one of several kinds of input, then shows the input for that choice. `choices` lists the kinds.

YAML key `choose`. Options: `choices`, `translation_key`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-color-rgb"><code>ha-selector-color_rgb</code></h3>

Home Assistant draws a native colour input (`ha-input` with `type="color"`). The value is a list of three numbers, for red, green and blue.

YAML key `color_rgb`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-color-temp"><code>ha-selector-color_temp</code></h3>

A slider for light colour temperature. `unit` sets the unit, and `min`, `max`, `min_mireds` and `max_mireds` set the range.

YAML key `color_temp`. Options: `unit`, `min`, `max`, `min_mireds`, `max_mireds`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-condition"><code>ha-selector-condition</code></h3>

Chooses a list of conditions, as in an automation. The value is a list of conditions.

YAML key `condition`. Options: `optionsInSidebar`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-config-entry"><code>ha-selector-config_entry</code></h3>

Chooses one configured integration entry. `integration` narrows the list to one integration.

YAML key `config_entry`. Options: `integration`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-constant"><code>ha-selector-constant</code></h3>

Not a field at all. It carries a fixed value in the schema and shows only its label or value as text, or nothing when disabled.

YAML key `constant`. Options: `value`, `label`, `translation_key`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-conversation-agent"><code>ha-selector-conversation_agent</code></h3>

Chooses a conversation agent, the thing that interprets what people say to Assist.

YAML key `conversation_agent`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-country"><code>ha-selector-country</code></h3>

Chooses a country. `countries` limits the list and `no_sort` keeps your order.

YAML key `country`. Options: `countries`, `no_sort`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-date"><code>ha-selector-date</code></h3>

A date field, in the format of the person’s language. See [Date & Time Picker](date-input.html).

YAML key `date`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-datetime"><code>ha-selector-datetime</code></h3>

A date field and a time field together. The value is one text string.

YAML key `datetime`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-device"><code>ha-selector-device</code></h3>

Chooses one or more devices. See [Device Picker](device-picker.html). `filter` narrows the list by integration, manufacturer, model or entity; `entity` and `multiple` also apply. To let people pick a Bluetooth, USB or discovered device, set `filter` to the `bluetooth`, `usb` or `zeroconf` integration, or use a text field when a free value is needed.

YAML key `device`. Options: `filter`, `entity`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-device-class"><code>ha-selector-device_class</code></h3>

Chooses a device class for one domain, such as the kinds of sensor. `domain` sets which domain’s classes are offered, and `multiple` allows more than one.

YAML key `device_class`. Options: `domain`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-duration"><code>ha-selector-duration</code></h3>

A length of time, in days, hours, minutes, seconds and milliseconds as you enable them. `enable_day`, `enable_millisecond`, `enable_second` and `allow_negative` switch parts on. See [Duration Input](duration-input.html). A duration is not a time of day.

YAML key `duration`. Options: `enable_day`, `enable_millisecond`, `allow_negative`, `enable_second`, `mode`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-entity"><code>ha-selector-entity</code></h3>

Chooses one or more entities. See [Entity Picker](entity-picker.html). `filter`, `include_entities` and `exclude_entities` narrow the list; `multiple` and `reorder` allow a list in a chosen order.

YAML key `entity`. Options: `multiple`, `include_entities`, `exclude_entities`, `filter`, `reorder`, `extra_options`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-entity-name"><code>ha-selector-entity_name</code></h3>

Chooses how an entity’s name is put together, from its floor, area, parent device, device and entity names, or from text you type. `entity_id` says which entity, and falls back to the entity of the form it sits in. `default_name` sets the starting choice, and `allow_custom` allows typed text.

YAML key `entity_name`. Options: `entity_id`, `default_name`, `allow_custom` (on by default).

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-file"><code>ha-selector-file</code></h3>

Uploads a file. `accept` limits the file types. See [File Upload / Dropzone](file-upload.html).

YAML key `file`. Options: `accept`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-floor"><code>ha-selector-floor</code></h3>

Chooses one or more floors. See [Floor Picker](floor-picker.html).

YAML key `floor`. Options: `entity`, `device`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-icon"><code>ha-selector-icon</code></h3>

Chooses an icon by name, with a preview. See [Icon Picker](icon-picker.html).

YAML key `icon`. Options: `placeholder`, `fallbackPath`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-label"><code>ha-selector-label</code></h3>

Chooses one or more labels. See [Label Picker](label-picker.html).

YAML key `label`. Options: `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-language"><code>ha-selector-language</code></h3>

Chooses a language. `languages` limits the list, `native_name` shows each language in its own name and `no_sort` keeps your order.

YAML key `language`. Options: `languages`, `native_name`, `no_sort`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-location"><code>ha-selector-location</code></h3>

Chooses a place on a map, with an optional radius. `radius`, `radius_readonly`, `icon`, `name` and `color` change how the place is drawn.

YAML key `location`. Options: `radius`, `radius_readonly`, `icon`, `name`, `color`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-media"><code>ha-selector-media</code></h3>

Chooses an item from a media source, with optional upload. `accept` limits the kinds, `image_upload` allows an upload and `multiple` allows several.

YAML key `media`. Options: `accept`, `image_upload`, `hide_content_type`, `content_id_helper`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-navigation"><code>ha-selector-navigation</code></h3>

Chooses a place to navigate to. `entity_id` and `area_id` give it context.

YAML key `navigation`. Options: `entity_id`, `area_id`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-number"><code>ha-selector-number</code></h3>

A number. With both `min` and `max` set it is a slider, with a number field beside it; otherwise it is a number box. `step`, `mode` (`slider` or `box`), `unit_of_measurement` and `slider_ticks` adjust it. In slider mode the label is plain text before the control, and the selector does not pass its label to the slider. Name the setting in the hint as well. See [Control Slider](sliders.html).

YAML key `number`. Options: `min`, `max`, `step`, `mode`, `unit_of_measurement`, `slider_ticks`, `translation_key`, `validation_message`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-numeric-threshold"><code>ha-selector-numeric_threshold</code></h3>

Chooses a number test for a trigger or condition. A drop-down gives the test: above, below, between or outside, with any change added in `changed` mode. Each value is either a number or an entity, with a unit list when `unit_of_measurement` offers more than one unit. `mode` sets the wording and the default test: `crossed` and `is` start at above, and `changed` starts at any change.

YAML key `numeric_threshold`. Options: `mode` (`crossed`, `changed` or `is`, with `crossed` the default), `unit_of_measurement`, `number`, `entity`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-object"><code>ha-selector-object</code></h3>

Takes a structured value, such as a list of items with several fields, entered through a YAML editor or a field list. `fields` describes each field, and `multiple` makes it a list.

YAML key `object`. Options: `label_field`, `description_field`, `translation_key`, `fields`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-period"><code>ha-selector-period</code></h3>

Chooses a period of time by name, such as `today`, `last_week` or `next_30d`. `options` lists the names to offer, from `today`, `yesterday`, `tomorrow`, `this_week`, `last_week`, `next_week`, `this_month`, `last_month`, `next_month`, `this_year`, `last_year`, `next_7d`, `next_30d` and `none`.

YAML key `period`. Options: `options`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-qr-code"><code>ha-selector-qr_code</code></h3>

Not an input. It shows a QR code for `data`. Always write the same information as text beside it.

YAML key `qr_code`. Options: `data`, `scale`, `error_correction_level`, `center_image`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-select"><code>ha-selector-select</code></h3>

Chooses from a fixed list. `options` lists the choices. A single choice in `list` mode is a group of radio buttons, and multiple choices in `list` mode are checkboxes. `box` mode shows large boxes. `dropdown` mode, or the default, shows a menu. `custom_value`, `multiple` or `reorder` give a field with chips. See [Dropdown Menu & Overflow Menu](select.html), [Radios](radios.html) and [Checkbox](checkboxes.html).

YAML key `select`. Options: `multiple`, `custom_value`, `mode`, `options`, `translation_key`, `sort`, `reorder`, `box_max_columns`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-selector"><code>ha-selector-selector</code></h3>

Chooses another selector. A drop-down gives the type, and that type’s own options appear under it. A manual choice takes the selector as YAML. The value is a selector, written as in the wrapper above.

YAML key `selector`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-serial-port"><code>ha-selector-serial_port</code></h3>

The serial port selector, `serial_port`, shows a list of ports with those it recommends marked, and a pencil button for typing a port by hand. The list is loaded only for an administrator, and only when the USB component is loaded. `extra_recommended_domains` adds integrations whose ports should be marked as recommended. The value is the port as text. For standalone guidance, use a labelled text field with a hint.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-state"><code>ha-selector-state</code></h3>

Chooses one or more states of an entity. `entity_id` and `attribute` say whose state, and `hide_states` leaves some out.

YAML key `state`. Options: `extra_options`, `entity_id`, `attribute`, `hide_states`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-state-class"><code>ha-selector-state_class</code></h3>

Chooses a state class for a sensor. `state_classes` limits the list, which otherwise holds the sensor state classes, and `multiple` allows more than one.

YAML key `state_class`. Options: `state_classes`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-statistic"><code>ha-selector-statistic</code></h3>

Chooses a long-term statistic, such as an energy meter. `device_class` narrows the list.

YAML key `statistic`. Options: `device_class`, `multiple`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-stt"><code>ha-selector-stt</code></h3>

Chooses a speech-to-text service, which turns speech into text for Assist.

YAML key `stt`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-target"><code>ha-selector-target</code></h3>

Chooses what an action acts on: entities, devices, areas, floors or labels. See [Entity, device and area pickers](../home-assistant/target-pickers.html).

YAML key `target`. Options: `entity`, `device`, `primary_entities_only`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-template"><code>ha-selector-template</code></h3>

A field for a template, written in a code editor, with an optional live preview. `preview` turns the preview on.

YAML key `template`. Options: `preview`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-text"><code>ha-selector-text</code></h3>

A text field. `multiline` gives a larger box, `multiple` a list of fields, and `type` sets the field type. `prefix`, `suffix`, `autocomplete`, `placeholder` and `pattern` refine it, and `validation_message` says what is wrong when the pattern fails. See [Text Input](text-input.html) and [Textarea](textarea.html).

YAML key `text`. Options: `multiline`, `type`, `prefix`, `suffix`, `placeholder`, `autocomplete`, `multiple`, `pattern`, `validation_message`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-theme"><code>ha-selector-theme</code></h3>

Chooses a theme by name.

YAML key `theme`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-time"><code>ha-selector-time</code></h3>

A time-of-day field. See [Date & Time Picker](date-input.html).

YAML key `time`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-timezone"><code>ha-selector-timezone</code></h3>

Chooses a time zone from a searchable list.

YAML key `timezone`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-trigger"><code>ha-selector-trigger</code></h3>

Chooses a list of triggers, as in an automation. The value is a list of triggers.

YAML key `trigger`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-tts"><code>ha-selector-tts</code></h3>

Chooses a text-to-speech service, which turns text into speech.

YAML key `tts`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-tts-voice"><code>ha-selector-tts_voice</code></h3>

Chooses a voice for a text-to-speech service.

YAML key `tts_voice`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-ui-action"><code>ha-selector-ui_action</code></h3>

Chooses what a tap, hold or double-tap does on a dashboard card. `actions` limits the list and `default_action` sets the first choice.

YAML key `ui_action`. Options: `actions`, `default_action`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-ui-clock-date-format"><code>ha-selector-ui_clock_date_format</code></h3>

Builds the date shown on a clock, from a list of day, month, year, weekday and separator parts. Each part added is a removable chip, and the value is the list of parts.

YAML key `ui_clock_date_format`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-ui-color"><code>ha-selector-ui_color</code></h3>

Chooses a colour. `default_color`, `include_none` and `include_state` add choices. See [Color Picker](color-picker.html).

YAML key `ui_color`. Options: `default_color`, `include_none`, `include_state`, `extra_options`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-ui-state-content"><code>ha-selector-ui_state_content</code></h3>

Chooses what to show for an entity’s state. `entity_id` says which entity, and `allow_name` and `allow_context` add extra choices.

YAML key `ui_state_content`. Options: `entity_id`, `allow_name`, `allow_context`.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-ui-time-format"><code>ha-selector-ui_time_format</code></h3>

Chooses how a time is shown: Auto, or one of the time formats Home Assistant lists, which are relative, total, date, time and date and time. Some formats also offer a short or long style.

YAML key `ui_time_format`. Options: none.

<h3 id="child-ha-selector--ha-selector-unit-of-measurement"><code>ha-selector-unit_of_measurement</code></h3>

Chooses a unit of measurement. `device_classes` and `state_classes` limit the units to those that fit, and `context` names other fields of the form whose device class or state class also limits them. When only one unit is allowed it is chosen for you. A unit that no longer fits after the form changes is cleared.

YAML key `unit_of_measurement`. Options: `device_classes`, `state_classes`, `context`.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One schema gives the right input for each kind of value, so forms stay consistent.
- Lists of devices, areas and entities come from the home itself, so people choose by name, not by identifier.
- A selector does not guarantee a label for assistive technology. Each input supplies its own, and some are not named by the selector’s label. In slider mode, for example, the label is plain text before the control and is not passed to the slider.
- In the service form the row heading is separate text beside the selector, so the heading and the control are not linked.
- Selectors that can reorder a list use a sortable list. Do not assume a keyboard way to reorder.
- Behaviour and options can differ between editors.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Choose the narrowest selector that fits the value. Use `duration` for a length of time and `time` for a time of day; a duration is not a time. Use `select` with few options and `device` or `entity` for homes with many. Give each field one plain label that names the value, put the hint before the field and say how a required field is marked, in words. Use plain option names. Use `autocomplete` on text fields that ask for personal details, and give a pattern a `validation_message` that says how to fix the value.

For standalone forms, where Home Assistant does not draw the input, use labelled native controls with a hint and an error message.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: each input needs a name that matches its visible label.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: say what is wanted before the field.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: a pattern should say what is wrong and how to fix it.
- 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose: text fields that collect personal details should set `autocomplete`.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: check each input you use.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: do not ask again for a value already given.
- 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: where a list can be reordered by dragging, a second way is needed.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the theme sets switches and buttons to 44 pixels.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check the inputs on your own screen.

One label, one hint and plain option names keep the effort low.

